  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A disturbance will bring the potential for \nheavy rain and flash flooding across the Piedmont on Friday \nafternoon and evening.\n\nA backdoor front will stall south of the region along the VA/NC\nborder tonight. This frontal boundary is providing a focal \npoint for storm activity to develop mainly across locations east\nof our forecast area. However, the boundary still trails \nsouthwest to just east of the Danville area. We could still see\nsome scattered showers/storms progressing through the evening \nhours across this location. Across the upper levels, the axis of\na 500mb trough looks to swing just west of the region across \nwestern Tennessee by Friday afternoon. As this happens, it will \nprovide additional forcing for ascent across the region. With \nnear 2.0 inch PWATs remaining in place across the Piedmonts of \nVA/NC Friday afternoon, there is a decent chance for flash \nflooding primarily east of the 29 corridor in Virginia and North\nCarolina. These PWATs combined with the stalled front and upper\nlevel forcing will lead to storms capable of producing 3-5 inch\nper hour rainfall rates at times, which if these storms train \nover the same areas they will likely lead to flash flooding \nconcerns. The largest flash flooding concerns look to be during \nthe later evening hours from 6pm to midnight for the areas \naforementioned above. Some uncertainty remains in the transient \nnature of storms tomorrow, as faster moving storms along the \nstalled boundary may lead to less chances for flash flooding. \nAlso, some CAM guidance keeps this boundary south of the area, \nwhich would also keep the heaviest rainfall out of the area \nleading to a much lower flash flood threat.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...After continued showers and thunderstorms on \nSaturday, drier weather returns Sunday and continues into early \nnext week.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms look to continue across the area on\nSaturday during the early morning hours across the Piedmont as\nthat backdoor front lifts north out of the region. During the\nafternoon hours, a cold front looks to approach the region from\nthe northwest, which will lead to some isolated shower and\nthunderstorm chances on Saturday afternoon and evening. By\nSunday, surface high pressure pushes into the region behind the\naforementioned cold front leading to much drier air over the\nregion through the middle of next week. \n\nThe aforementioned surface high pressure system looks to\neventually push east off the Mid-Atlantic states coastlines \ninto the western Atlantic leading to the return of warm moist \nair and daily scattered shower and thunderstorm chances.\n\nTemperatures overall look to remain near normal for this time of\nyear, with highs in the mid to upper 80s and low 90s across the\nPiedmont, and upper 70s to low 80s west of the Blue Ridge.",
